const COURSE = {
 title:'Robot Mission Lab', subtitle:'Build your first delivery rover', grade:'Grades 6–8', schedule:'8 modules · 16 × 45-minute sessions',
 intro:'Build a robot that can deliver a small paper parcel, detect an obstacle, and stop at its destination. Work like an engineer: predict, build, test, explain, and improve.',
 kit:'BBC micro:bit V2 + Kitronik :MOVE Motor (5683)',
 setup:[
 'For each pair: one micro:bit V2, one Kitronik :MOVE Motor (5683), four AA batteries of the type recommended by Kitronik, a data-capable micro-USB cable, and a computer with MakeCode access. The micro:bit is sold separately from some robot kits.',
 'Shared supplies: ruler or tape measure, masking tape, large white paper, dark tape, cardboard, paper, reusable adhesive, scissors used under school procedures, and an upright cardboard obstacle at least as wide as the robot. Use a flat floor test lane, away from feet and stairs.',
 'Teacher preflight: assemble the supplied kit using its manufacturer guide. Fit the supplied parts as directed; this course adds a removable paper cargo cradle rather than rebuilding the electronics or chassis. Insert the micro:bit only with robot power off and in the manufacturer’s stated orientation.',
 'At makecode.microbit.org create a project. Open Extensions, search “move motor”, and choose Kitronik’s kitronik-move-motor extension. For the supplied reference program, switch to JavaScript, replace the starter text with the lesson’s .ts file, then switch to Blocks if desired. Set the indicated tuning values before downloading.',
 'With robot battery power off, transfer the program by USB using MakeCode’s device connection or copy its downloaded .hex file onto the MICROBIT drive. Disconnect the USB cable before floor driving. Use the robot’s specified AA supply for driving; do not connect a separate micro:bit battery pack at the same time.',
 'Start on a clear floor with an adult ready to switch off the robot. Button A starts a trial and button B requests a software stop. The physical power switch is the immediate stop if code or controls fail. Reference programs also have a short time limit. Test each program on the actual kit before class; wheel motion and stopping distances require local calibration.',
 'Students rotate driver/programmer and observer/data-recorder roles after each trial. Keep hair, fingers and loose clothing away from wheels. Switch power off before adjusting the robot; keep liquids and metal objects away from the board. Follow the school’s and manufacturers’ safety instructions.'
 ],
 routine:['Session A: launch and predict (5 min), explain/model (10), build and program (20), first test (5), save and tidy (5).','Session B: retrieval (5 min), fair tests and revisions (25), explanation or peer review (10), exit ticket and tidy (5).'],
 lessons:[
 {id:1,title:'Sense, decide, respond',mission:'Build a rover that responds to its operator',concept:'A robot is a programmable physical system that acts on its environment. Some robots are remotely operated; others use sensor readings to act autonomously. In this course, sense–decide–act is a useful design model, not a rule that excludes every remotely controlled robot.',objective:'Identify an input, a programmed decision and an output, then demonstrate a predictable response in three trials.',parts:'Assembled rover, micro:bit, USB cable and computer. Keep motor power off until the teacher checks the program.',build:['Draw the rover and label the micro:bit controller, a button input, the ultrasonic sensor, motors and wheels. Show the direction of information flow.','Open lesson 1 in MakeCode. Predict the micro:bit display after A and after B. This program deliberately keeps both motors stopped.','Download and test on the mounted controller. Press A, then B; the display changes from a tick to a cross. Treat this as the rover’s operator interface.','Change the ready display to your team’s original five-by-five icon. Keep the stop response. Explain which part of the program interprets the button event.'],success:'A shows the chosen ready icon, B shows the stop icon, and wheels remain still in all three trials.',variables:'Input: button A or B. Observe: display and wheel state. Keep the same program between trials.',columns:['Trial / button','Predicted output','Observed output','Matches?'],question:'A remotely operated exploration robot is still a robot. What extra capability would make this rover autonomous?',answer:'It would read an environmental sensor and use a programmed rule to choose an action without a person choosing every movement.',misconception:'“Think” means execute programmed rules; it does not imply that a micro:bit understands or has intentions.',fix:'If the display stays unchanged, check that the new program transferred and that you are pressing the correct button. If a motor moves, switch off and load the correct lesson program.',extend:'Add a distinct icon for A+B. Explain why a recognizable stop state helps another operator.',narration:'A robot takes inputs, follows instructions, and produces an action. Today you will build its operator interface. Predict the display before you press a button. A good engineer can explain why the robot responded.'},
 {id:2,title:'Motor checkout',mission:'Make a controlled, repeatable drive',concept:'Each drive wheel has a motor. Two similar wheel speeds move the rover approximately straight; unequal speeds create a turn. A speed setting is a motor command, not a measurement in centimetres per second.',objective:'Run a bounded forward drive, demonstrate stop, and identify two sources of variation.',parts:'Rover, ruler, floor tape and clear 1.5 m test lane. Teacher first tests with wheels clear of the floor, holding only the chassis.',build:['With power off, identify both motors and the physical switch. Check wheels are secure and nothing rubs. Do not disconnect factory wiring.','Mark a start line. Set the reference program to speed 25 and drive time 800 milliseconds. These are starting values, not calibrated distances.','Place the rover behind the line. Press A from a safe position. Observe the automatic stop; record travel distance. Repeat three times without changing the code.','Run again and press B during motion. Verify the software stop. Keep the power switch accessible; it is the immediate fallback.','If travel curves, first check the surface, wheels and batteries. Change only one cause or setting, then repeat.'],success:'Three automatic stops and a demonstrated B stop, with travel distance and variation recorded.',variables:'Change only one factor, such as commanded speed. Keep time, start direction, floor and load fixed.',columns:['Trial / speed setting','Time (ms)','Distance (cm)','Drift / stop notes'],question:'Why might speed 25 produce a different distance tomorrow?',answer:'Battery condition, surface friction, wheel alignment or payload may change; the command is not a guaranteed physical speed.',misconception:'Equal speed commands do not guarantee perfectly equal wheel motion.',fix:'If motors do not run, check robot batteries and switch; USB alone is not the rover drive supply. If direction is wrong, stop and check the correct extension and program.',extend:'Compare two low speed settings with three trials each. Use mean distance to estimate the difference.',narration:'Your first drive should be short and controlled. Start with a low speed, keep a clear lane, and check that the stop works. Measure the distance instead of assuming that the speed number tells you how far the rover will go.'},
 {id:3,title:'Design a cargo cradle',mission:'Carry a paper parcel without blocking the robot',concept:'A chassis supports components. An attachment changes how weight is distributed and may obstruct sensors or wheels. Engineers compare designs against constraints, rather than assuming that a stronger-looking structure performs better.',objective:'Build and compare two removable cargo cradles using consistent trials and a stated design constraint.',parts:'Rover, folded paper parcel, lightweight cardboard, reusable adhesive, ruler and scissors. Teacher approves attachment before driving.',build:['Make the same lightweight folded-paper parcel for both designs. Do not add metal weights. Sketch a low cradle that leaves the ultrasonic front, underside sensors, wheels, buttons and power switch clear.','Build a removable cradle on the available upper mounting area, following manufacturer restrictions. Do not tape across circuitry, wiring or sensor faces. Teacher checks the fit with power off.','Use lesson 3’s short drive. Test the same distance/time setting three times. Record whether the parcel stays in place and whether the attachment rubs or tips.','Revise one feature, such as the height of a paper retaining wall or cradle position. Repeat on the same floor with the same parcel and code.','Choose a design using your results. Draw its attachment points so another team can reproduce it.'],success:'Parcel stays in place for three short trials; the sensor view, wheels, controls and switch remain unobstructed.',variables:'Change one cradle feature. Keep parcel, surface, start position and program fixed.',columns:['Design / trial','Parcel retained?','Any rubbing or tipping?','Evidence / revision'],question:'Why should you keep the parcel and code unchanged when comparing the cradles?',answer:'It helps isolate the effect of the cradle design; changing several factors would make the cause of a difference unclear.',misconception:'The supplied chassis is already built. Bracing a cardboard attachment is different from redesigning the robot’s electronics.',fix:'If the attachment shifts or interferes, stop and remove it. Reduce size or reposition it; do not compensate by increasing speed.',extend:'Have another pair reproduce your attachment from the drawing, then compare results.',narration:'Today your rover becomes a delivery vehicle. Build a light cargo cradle and leave its wheels, sensors, and power switch clear. Test the same paper parcel before and after one design change. Let your evidence choose the better design.'},
 {id:4,title:'Give the rover reliable senses',mission:'Build and calibrate a parking-distance display',concept:'An ultrasonic sensor estimates distance from an echo. Readings can be affected by object angle, shape and surface. A missing or invalid reading is not evidence that the route is clear. Calibration compares readings with a reference measurement.',objective:'Compare distance readings at three measured positions and explain one unreliable reading.',parts:'Rover, ruler, upright flat cardboard wall and test lane. Motors stay off for this activity.',build:['Keep wheels stationary. Use the manufacturer’s centimetre unit setting in lesson 4. The micro:bit shows the measured distance; display scrolling is acceptable here because the rover is not driving.','Measure from the front sensor face to the cardboard wall. Place it at 10, 20 and 30 cm. At each location, take three readings and record the variation.','Tilt the wall or substitute a soft object. Observe whether readings change. Do not point the sensor at faces or use people as obstacles.','Choose a warning threshold such as 20 cm for a later low-speed approach. Explain why real braking distance must be tested separately.','Trace the information path: distance sensor → controller comparison → display now, motors later.'],success:'Nine readings recorded; measured and reported distances compared; one limitation explained.',variables:'First vary measured distance only. Then vary the target angle while holding distance fixed.',columns:['Measured distance (cm)','Readings 1 / 2 / 3','Difference / variation','Surface or angle'],question:'The sensor returns zero. Should your moving robot assume there is no obstacle?',answer:'No. Treat a zero or invalid reading as uncertain and stop; test the target position and sensor before continuing.',misconception:'A sensor reading is an estimate, not perfect knowledge of the environment.',fix:'Use a wide, upright flat target in front of the sensor. Check for a blocked sensor or low batteries. Never solve a missing reading by removing the stop rule.',extend:'Calculate the mean of each three-reading set and compare it with the ruler value.',narration:'Sensors provide evidence, but the evidence can be imperfect. Compare the rover’s distance reading with a ruler. Try three positions and repeat each measurement. A zero reading means we should investigate, not assume that the path is safe.'},
 {id:5,title:'Code a delivery route',mission:'Drive to a marked delivery bay',concept:'A sequence runs commands in order. Timed movement is open-loop: the program does not check where the rover actually ended up. A turn duration is not a guaranteed angle. Calibrate movement on the same floor used for the mission.',objective:'Program a two-leg route and use three trials to tune one timing variable.',parts:'Rover, empty cargo cradle, floor tape and ruler. Mark a generous delivery bay, starting at least 30 cm from any obstacle.',build:['In the online Mission Lab, predict a route before running it. Try forward, turn and stop commands. The grid is a logic model, not a physical calibration tool.','On the floor, mark a start, a turn point and a delivery bay. Sketch the intended route with arrow directions.','Open lesson 5. Start with short forward times and a low-speed timed spin; test the turn alone before combining the route. A 400 ms spin is only an initial tuning value.','Run the two-leg route three times. Measure how far the stopping point is from the bay centre. Change only turn time or one drive time, then retest.','Place a repeat block around a short segment only if your chosen route repeats. Explain why blindly repeating an inaccurate segment compounds error.'],success:'A documented two-leg route ends within the team’s stated bay tolerance in at least two of three trials, and stops every time.',variables:'Change one timing value. Keep speeds, floor, start alignment and load fixed.',columns:['Trial / timing change','End error (cm)','Turn observation','Inside bay?'],question:'Why can a route succeed once but miss the bay on the next run?',answer:'Open-loop timing cannot correct for differences in traction, batteries, start direction or load; repeated trials reveal reliability.',misconception:'A “turn right” instruction in a grid represents a precise quarter-turn; a real motor needs calibration or feedback.',fix:'Shorten the sequence and test its legs separately. Check the start direction before changing the code.',extend:'Compare a long route with a shorter route. Keep a record of program length and successful trials.',narration:'A route is a sequence of choices. Plan the turns before you drive. In the simulator, a turn is exact. On the floor, your rover needs calibration. Measure where it stops, change one timing value, and test again.'},
 {id:6,title:'Stop before an obstacle',mission:'Build a cautious autonomous approach',concept:'A conditional chooses an action from sensor evidence. Your rover repeatedly compares distance with a threshold. It stops on a close or invalid reading. Sampling, movement and braking take time, so a threshold must include a tested margin.',objective:'Demonstrate a sensor-controlled stop before contact in three trials, then explain the threshold using data.',parts:'Rover, wide upright cardboard wall, ruler and clear floor lane. No people, animals, stairs or table edges as targets.',build:['Start at least 50 cm from the cardboard wall. Use low speed and an initial threshold of 20 cm. Teacher pretests and increases the threshold if needed.','Predict which action occurs for distances of 30 cm, 15 cm and zero. Test that reasoning with the Mission Lab’s “if blocked” command.','Load lesson 6. Press A. The rover approaches, samples distance repeatedly and stops when the reading is at or below the threshold or invalid.','Measure the actual remaining gap after each of three trials. Record any contact as a failed test. Do not hold the target by hand during motion.','Improve one parameter. If stopping is late or inconsistent, lower speed and increase the margin, testing changes separately. Keep the invalid-reading stop.'],success:'Three stops before contact, each with a measured gap, plus an explanation of one failure or limitation.',variables:'Change threshold OR speed, never both in the same comparison.',columns:['Trial / threshold (cm)','Speed command','Final gap (cm)','Contact / observation'],question:'Why might increasing the stopping threshold help a rover that reacts too late?',answer:'It starts the stop decision farther from the obstacle, allowing margin for sensing and motor response. It still needs real tests.',misconception:'Obstacle detection does not make this a safety-certified device. The teacher retains direct supervision and switch access.',fix:'If a reading is zero, stop and recheck the sensor view. If the program continues after stopping, check the latched stopped state and that no second routine restarts motors.',extend:'Add an on-screen reason for the stop: obstacle, invalid reading, operator stop or time limit.',narration:'Your rover will now respond to the world. A distance reading selects an action: move cautiously or stop. Test the rule before you run it. Record the real stopping gap and keep a margin for imperfect readings and motor response.'},
 {id:7,title:'Plan the delivery mission',mission:'Deliver a paper parcel using a tested approach',concept:'A mission combines requirements, constraints and evidence. Reliable behaviour is more valuable than speed. A state is a named stage such as ready, approaching or stopped; only one stage should control the motors at a time.',objective:'Combine a cargo attachment, a bounded route and a sensor stop into a mission with measurable acceptance criteria.',parts:'Rover, approved paper cradle and parcel, floor bay, flat cardboard stop target, ruler and worksheet.',build:['Write a mission brief: carry the same paper parcel, follow a clear approach lane and stop in a marked bay before the flat target. Set the bay using the stopping gaps measured in lesson 6.','Assign rotating roles and sketch the lane. List hazards and the immediate stop method. Teacher approves the setup.','Use lesson 7’s reference approach program and your measured speed and threshold. Start with a straight approach; add a timed turn only after testing it separately.','Run three trials with identical starting conditions. Record parcel retained, bay reached and no contact. A trial passes only when all three are true.','Choose one improvement from the evidence. Retest three times and compare success counts; keep unsuccessful results in your log.'],success:'At least two of three full mission trials meet all three criteria; every trial remains bounded and supervised.',variables:'Choose one deliberate change between trial sets, then keep it fixed within each set.',columns:['Trial / version','Parcel retained?','In bay / no contact?','Pass? Evidence'],question:'Your first version passed 1 of 3 trials and your revision passed 2 of 3. What can you claim?',answer:'The revision performed better in these small trial sets. More trials are needed before claiming broad reliability; do not claim it always works.',misconception:'A visually impressive run is not enough; the criteria must be set before testing.',fix:'If the combined mission fails, test cargo retention, movement and sensing separately to locate the failure.',extend:'Use the optional line-sensor investigation in the teacher guide to design a future navigation improvement; no unsupported claim that the current rover follows a line.',narration:'Put your tested parts together. Define what a successful delivery means before the run. Keep the same starting conditions, record every attempt, and improve one thing at a time. A reliable delivery matters more than a fast one.'},
 {id:8,title:'Showcase with evidence',mission:'Prove, explain and hand over your robot',concept:'An engineering handover lets another person understand, operate and evaluate a design. Clear documentation includes the actual limits and remaining problems. A demonstration shows one run; a test log shows what happened across runs.',objective:'Present a reproducible demonstration and defend a design choice using measurements and a limitation.',parts:'Final rover, unchanged mission layout, saved program, engineering log and presentation worksheet.',build:['Freeze a named program version and draw the final layout. Write setup, start, stop and reset instructions for another pair.','Run three final acceptance trials with the same parcel and target. Record all results before selecting a demonstration clip or photo.','Have another pair follow your instructions under teacher supervision. Observe unclear steps and revise the handover without changing the test data.','Give a two-minute presentation: need and criteria; sense–decide–act explanation; one design change with before/after evidence; one limitation and next test.','Complete a peer review and individual reflection. Save the MakeCode project and export the worksheet or log. Teacher assesses each student’s explanation as well as team performance.'],success:'A reproducible handover, three recorded trials, a two-minute evidence-based explanation and an individual reflection.',variables:'Keep the final design fixed during acceptance tests. Label any later change as a new version.',columns:['Final trial','All criteria passed?','Observed failure / gap','Next test'],question:'What should you say if the showcase demonstration fails?',answer:'Describe what happened, stop safely, compare it with the prior trial record and identify a specific next test. Never hide or replace the recorded result.',misconception:'Engineering success includes finding and explaining limits, not only achieving a perfect demonstration.',fix:'Use the saved program and setup drawing to reproduce conditions. If time is short, demonstrate a verified subsystem and report the full mission’s limits honestly.',extend:'Propose one additional user requirement, explain its tradeoff and design a test without claiming the feature is already implemented.',narration:'Your final job is to explain the evidence. Show what your robot can do, how you tested it, and what still needs work. Give another team clear operating instructions. Engineers earn trust by making their results understandable and reproducible.'}
 ],
 rubric:[['Mission and constraints','Defines measurable criteria, a clear floor setup and safe start/stop procedures.'],['Program and explanation','Explains inputs, decisions and outputs; identifies a sequence and a conditional accurately.'],['Testing and iteration','Records repeated trials, changes one factor, compares evidence and reports limitations.'],['Communication and collaboration','Provides a reproducible handover and an individual explanation; shares roles fairly.']],
 sources:[['Original course','https://luvbuniz.github.io/robotics/'],['Kit and assembly resources','https://kitronik.co.uk/products/5683-kitronik-move-motor-for-the-bbc-micro-bit'],['Manufacturer MakeCode extension','https://makecode.microbit.org/pkg/KitronikLtd/pxt-kitronik-move-motor'],['micro:bit safety','https://microbit.org/get-started/user-guide/electrical-product-guidance/'],['MakeCode editor','https://makecode.microbit.org/']]
};
if (typeof module !== 'undefined') module.exports = COURSE;
